🔹 1. Pong (Classic)
What you’ll learn:

2D physics (basic)

Input handling (player movement)

Collision detection

Score tracking

Simple AI (for single-player)

Why it's good:
Very simple mechanics, easy to implement and expand on (like adding sound or power-ups).

🔹 2. Breakout / Brick Breaker
What you’ll learn:

Instancing scenes (for the bricks)

Signals (when ball hits a brick)

Ball movement and physics

Game states (win/lose)

Why it's good:
Slightly more complex than Pong but still manageable, and it introduces object management and instancing.

🔹 3. Top-down Collecting Game
(E.g., player moves around to collect coins/stars) What you’ll learn:

Tilemaps (optional)

Input movement

Collision detection

UI updates (score counter)

Signals and Area2D

Why it's good:
You’ll start to understand player movement in a 2D space and how to build simple objectives.

🔹 4. Platformer (Mario-style)
What you’ll learn:

KinematicBody2D / CharacterBody2D

Gravity & jumping logic

AnimationPlayer or AnimatedSprite2D

Tilemaps for levels

Traps, enemies, collectibles

Why it's good:
This one’s a bit more intermediate but still very doable. You’ll learn how to handle physics and character mechanics.

🔹 5. Clicker / Idle Game
What you’ll learn:

GUI elements

Buttons and signals

Resource management (like a currency)

Basic logic for upgrades

Why it's good:
No physics, no complex movement — pure logic and UI work. A great intro to game logic and UI systems.

🔹 6. Memory Match / Card Flip Game
What you’ll learn:

Scene instancing

Shuffling/randomization

Matching logic

Simple animations or flip effects

Game restart and state management

Why it's good:
It’s fun and teaches you about game state, user interaction, and tracking object data.

🔹 Bonus: "Dodge the Creeps" (Official Godot Tutorial)
Godot's official tutorial game where you:

Move around to avoid enemies

Track score over time

Learn about timers, spawning, and signals

📌 Tutorial link: It's in the official Godot docs and YouTube channel — super beginner friendly.
